WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.670
Game of Thrones provided unforgettable moments,
near-perfect episodes, and stunning seasons

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.020
throughout its run, but as it continued, it
also changed television in many ways, including

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.680
a few more subtle changes that flew slightly
under the radar.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.980
As Game of Thrones started its run, it seemed
to have a clear main character that viewers

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.060
were supposed to rally behind, and that character
was Ned Stark.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.380
As the honorable patriarch of the highborn
Stark family, Ned, the Warden of the North,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.390
was lured away from his family and life at
Winterfell to head to King's Landing, the

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.880
capital of Westeros, to become the Hand of
the King.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.740
In doing so, he splinters the Stark family
across the map of Westeros, leaving his wife

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.400
and sons behind as he and his daughters make
a new home in King's Landing.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.680
However, things take a turn for the worse
when Ned discovers that the heirs to the throne

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.769
aren't actually the children of King Robert
Baratheon, but are instead the children of

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:50.260
Queen Cersei and her brother.

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:55.020
In the penultimate episode of the first season,
he confesses to his "crimes" in front of the

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.160
entire populace of King's Landing, ultimately
losing his head anyway.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.940
Fans of the books saw this moment coming,
but for those who had just started with the

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:07.530
show, it remains one of the best moments on
Thrones, perfectly setting up expectations

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.380
before yanking the rug out from under the
entire audience.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.640
From older films like Ben-Hur to more recent
epics like the Lord of the Rings trilogy,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.180
movies have always been afforded the budgets
and scope to stage enormous action sequences,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.660
but that opportunity has largely been absent
from television.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.460
Even prestige dramas like Breaking Bad and
Mad Men, while beautifully shot, remained

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.660
relatively modest in scope, but all of that
changed with Game of Thrones.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.810
Easily the most technically ambitious show
in television history, Thrones' filming spanned

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.680
continents, shooting in exotic locales like
Iceland and Croatia as it gave fans bigger

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.140
and bigger sequences.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.400
"Those are brave men knocking at our door.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.120
Let's go kill them!"

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.980
While relatively low budgets during season
one prevented the showrunners from shooting

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.440
battle scenes, that all changed with season
two's "Blackwater" which led to even more

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.290
expensive sequences down the line.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.080
"Battle of the Bastards," the climax of Season
Six, set new standards, and in the show's

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.420
final season, the Battle of Winterfell ended
up becoming the longest ever battle shot for

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.900
television or film.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.700
For years, television couldn't compete with
film on an epic scale.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.300
Thrones proved it was not only possible, but
could yield some pretty unforgettable results.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.780
Before Game of Thrones, it probably seemed
like a sprawling, dense fantasy series stood

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.020
little chance of becoming HBO's biggest hit
of all time.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.760
Shortly after the show's premiere in 2011,
however, the network clearly realized that

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.940
they had a massive success on their hands
and got to work making sure that the showrunners

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.680
had as much money as they needed.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.500
"And you will be speaking to me with more
respect."

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.770
Much of the added expense went to battle sequences,
with the Battle of Blackwater Bay bumping

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:55.220
the show up to a staggering $8 million for
the episode.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.520
Even then, showrunners reportedly had to fight
almost as hard as their characters to get

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.260
that increase.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.240
However, by the time the show reached its
final season in 2019, it was breaking records

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:11.180
once again with a budget increase that brought
each episode to a whopping $15 million, and

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.280
some episodes, like the one featuring the
Battle of Winterfell, may have even surpassed

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:15.780
that.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:21.120
In recent years, $10 million budgets for prestige
TV have been doled out more commonly, but

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:26.511
a budget for the final season that reached
$90 million is pretty unprecedented, making

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.020
it clear that HBO would spare no expense when
the time came to finish off their signature

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:31.520
show.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.720
With massive budgets reportedly already set
for the spinoffs, it looks like HBO has few

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:37.240
regrets.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.060
For the first five seasons of the show, Game
of Thrones stuck pretty faithfully to its

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:46.810
source material, the novel series A Song of
Ice and Fire, by George R.R. Martin, which

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.620
is currently comprised of five books.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.620
Named for the first book in the series, Thrones
tackled all of its biggest moments, including

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:59.080
Ned Stark's execution, the infamous Red Wedding,
the doomed wedding of Joffrey Baratheon, and

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.160
Jon Snow's death, before moving past its source
material in season six.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.500
Season six came on the heels of a particularly
brutal fifth season, which contained some

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.640
of the most hard-to-watch scenes in the series.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.680
Many critics and fans believed that once the
show could no longer follow Martin's strict

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.180
plot map, even though he was a frequent consultant
on the show, the quality suffered.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:23.580
Whether or not it was truly able to hold its
own against Martin's books, it still marks

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.220
a turning point for adaptations.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.180
Source material is almost always finished
by the time adaptations are filmed, so for

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.870
the showrunners to even attempt to move beyond
Martin's books while still maintaining the

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.560
story was a massive feat.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.760
"Shock value" will always be synonymous with
Game of Thrones.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.460
Viewers were traumatized by the Red Wedding
and horrified by Hardhome, but they could

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:49.400
also cheer as Jon and Sansa defeated Ramsay
Bolton at the Battle of the Bastards, as the

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.690
Night King met his defeat at the Battle of
Winterfell, or as Arya changed faces to get

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.180
her revenge on Walder Frey.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.820
"Tell them the North remembers.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.720
Tell them winter came for House Frey."

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:09.020
That being said, this shock value wasn't always
the show's most valuable or even enjoyable

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:09.520
quality.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.760
Throughout its run, it pushed new boundaries
for what viewers could withstand.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.430
The Red Wedding was bad enough, but watching
multiple episodes featuring graphic torture

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:24.060
scenes or the show's many instances of assault
as a plot device often felt like too much.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.400
Even so, Thrones set new standards for shock
value, and if future ambitious showrunners

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.170
can learn something from its triumphs and
its failures in equal measure, it can only

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.900
improve the television landscape.

